<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rifa'a al-Tahtawi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rifa'a at-Tahtawi, Rifa'a Rafi' al-Tahtawi, Rifaa al-Tahtawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rifa'a al-Tahtawi was a 19th-century Egyptian writer, teacher and translator, an early figure of the Nahda, the Arab renaissance. A scholar of al-Azhar, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1826 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> to a student mission sent by Muhammad Ali and read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> there; his account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Takhlis al-ibriz fi talkhis Bariz, appeared in 1834. In 1835 he founded the School of Languages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and he supervised the translation of more than 2,000 foreign works into Arabic. His guide to educating girls and boys alike was published in 1873.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rifaa-al-tahtawi.jpg" alt="Rifa'a al-Tahtawi" width="150" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahtawi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tahtawi was born in 1801 in the village of Tahta in Sohag, the year French troops evacuated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He studied at al-Azhar, and his teacher and mentor Hasan al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Attar</a> recommended him as chaplain to a group of students whom Muhammad Ali sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1826. He was meant to serve as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, but was allowed to associate with the other members of the mission. Such missions went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the early 19th century to study arts and sciences and to acquire technical skills such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, shipbuilding and modern military techniques.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In France Tahtawi studied ethics, social and political philosophy, mathematics and geometry, and read Condillac, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> and Bézout. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1831 to join the effort to modernise infrastructure and education. In 1835 he founded the School of Languages, also called the School of Translators, which became part of Ain Shams University in 1973. It graduated the earliest modern Egyptian intellectual milieu. Tahtawi translated and supervised translations of military manuals, geography and European history, over 2,000 foreign works in all. In the late 1860s he sat on the Educational Council of the new Ministry of Education, and from 1870 to 1873 he edited its magazine Rawdat al-Madaris. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1873.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His account of his stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Takhlis al-ibriz fi talkhis Bariz, appeared in 1834 and made him one of the first Egyptian scholars to write about Western culture, seeking understanding between Islamic and Christian civilisations. His writings on political and moral philosophy introduced Egyptian readers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas of secular authority, political rights and liberty, and to ideas of the public good and of what made a civilised or good Egyptian. He also wrote a history of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and translated French civil law in 1866 and trade law in 1868. The Honest Guide for the Education of Girls and Boys, published in 1873, answered a request from the Ministry of Education for a book on pedagogy for both sexes.</p><p>He is counted among the early Islamic modernists, arguing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>'s principles were compatible with European modernity, and his work influenced later scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a>. Shaden Tageldin has argued that his admiration for French culture encouraged an intellectual colonisation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rifa'a at-Tahtawi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Love of the homeland, the watan, became a theme: <b>Rifa’a al-Tahtawi</b> wrote of patriotic duty in Egypt, and Butrus al-Bustani appealed to the people of Syria, Christian, Muslim and Druze, to see themselves as children of one country.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Reformers at the turn of the 19th century oscillated between admiration for Western progress and rejection of it; Rifa‘a <b>al-Tahtawi</b> (d. 1872) exemplified the former, ‘Abd al-Rahman al-Jabarti (d. 1825) the latter.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-islamism">Pan-Islamism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first printing came in 1857, at the Bulaq Press in Cairo, in a standalone volume prepared by Naṣr al-Hūrīnī with crucial support from <b>Rifa'a at-Tahtawi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/muqaddimah">Muqaddimah · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tahta, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.77,31.5&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Tahta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5380 1181 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rifa'a al-Tahtawi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5830 1451h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5830 1451h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.77,31.5&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
